#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# trig_expand

#% text
# `trig_expand(e)` expands sines and cosines of sums and integer multiples by the addition theorems.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `sin(a + b)`, `cos(a + b)` and `sin(n*x)`, `cos(n*x)` for integers `n` become polynomials in the sines and cosines of the parts; the result is expanded.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
trig_expand(sin(2*x))
#% output
#= 2*cos(x)*sin(x)

#% cell
trig_expand(cos(x + y))
#% output
#= cos(x)*cos(y) - sin(x)*sin(y)

#% cell
trig_expand(sin(3*x))
#% output
#= -sin(x)^3 + 3*cos(x)^2*sin(x)

#% cell
trig_expand(cos(2*x)^2)
#% output
#= -2*cos(x)^2*sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^4 + sin(x)^4

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Hyperbolic functions:

#% cell
trig_expand(sinh(x + y))
#% output
#= cosh(x)*sinh(y) + cosh(y)*sinh(x)

#% text
# Sums of three angles:

#% cell
trig_expand(sin(a + b + c))
#% output
#= cos(a)*cos(b)*sin(c) + cos(a)*cos(c)*sin(b) + cos(b)*cos(c)*sin(a) - sin(a)*sin(b)*sin(c)

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `trig_reduce` goes the other way:

#% cell
trig_reduce(trig_expand(cos(3*x)))
#% output
#= cos(3*x)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `trig_reduce`, `trig_to_exp`, `expand`, `simplify`
